Pre-diabetes (Impaired Fasting Glucose / Impaired Glucose Tolerance)
Endocrine & Metabolic Disorders
Often asymptomatic, Central obesity, Acanthosis nigricans, Impaired fasting glucose/HbA1c

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: EMD-038
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe, generalized fatigue, frequent nighttime urination, and a superficial skin infection in a skin fold.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute deterioration in the patient's energy levels causing them to be bedbound, accompanied by severe nocturia disrupting sleep, and a painful, spreading rash in the groin. Immediate out-of-hours assessment was required to evaluate for an acute, severe hyperglycaemic state, rule out spreading cellulitis, and initiate appropriate antibiotic and metabolic management before routine clinics opened.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I have zero energy, I'm getting up five times a night to pee, and the rash in my groin is burning and spreading."
History of Present Illness: Over the past two weeks, the patient reports a significant escalation in mild generalized fatigue, culminating in profound lethargy tonight that has left them unable to perform household chores. They report waking up 4 to 5 times per night to urinate (nocturia), which is severely impacting their sleep. For the past three days, they have developed a painful, erythematous, and pruritic rash in the right inguinal crease. The patient notes a gradual increase in central abdominal obesity with a significantly increased waist circumference over the past year. They report a strong family history of type 2 diabetes mellitus but have never been formally tested.
Functional Status: Currently resting in bed due to profound fatigue and pain from the groin intertrigo.
Social/Safety Concerns: Poor sleep hygiene due to nocturia. Risk of secondary systemic infection from the skin breakdown.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/88 mmHg | HR: 82 bpm | Temp: 37.1 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 98 percent on room air | Wt: 105 Kg
General: Overweight, fatigued, and uncomfortable when mobilizing.
Physical Exam: Abdominal exam reveals prominent central adiposity. Dermatological exam reveals a bright red, macerated, weeping erythematous plaque in the right inguinal crease consistent with intertrigo, likely complicated by candidiasis. Prominent acanthosis nigricans (velvety hyperpigmented plaques) is noted in the skin folds of the neck and axillae. Multiple skin tags (acrochordons) are present around the neck. Cardiovascular exam confirms new-onset mild hypertension. Bedside capillary blood glucose taken randomly two hours post-dinner is 9.8 mmol/L. Diagnostic tetrad features observed: Central obesity, Acanthosis nigricans, suspected Impaired fasting glucose/HbA1c (indicated by the borderline high random glucose and symptoms), previously Often asymptomatic until this acute decompensation.
Environmental Observations: Evidence of a diet heavily reliant on processed carbohydrates and sugary beverages in the kitchen.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular prescribed medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Pre-diabetes / Metabolic Syndrome presenting with symptomatic hyperglycaemia and acute candidal intertrigo - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Frank Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The random blood glucose of 9.8 mmol/L is highly suspicious but not definitively above the >11.1 mmol/L threshold for an acute random diagnosis. Formal fasting venous bloods are required, but the metabolic trajectory is clear.

Primary Bacterial Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The rash is strictly confined to the opposing skin folds, has a classic beefy red appearance with satellite lesions typical of candida, and lacks spreading unilateral streaking or high fever.

Urinary Tract Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of dysuria, haematuria, or suprapubic pain, pointing to osmotic diuresis (sugar in urine) as the cause of the nocturia.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute functional decline due to severe fatigue and sleep disruption, complicated by an opportunistic fungal skin infection. Triage accurately flagged the nocturia and spreading rash. Objective assessment confirmed clinical stigmata of profound insulin resistance (acanthosis, central obesity) and a borderline elevated capillary blood glucose, identifying the root cause of the fatigue, diuresis, and fungal susceptibility. Delaying assessment forced the patient to endure a painful, worsening skin infection and severe sleep deprivation. Immediate out-of-hours evaluation was clinically mandated to treat the acute intertrigo, assess current glycaemic safety, and coordinate definitive metabolic testing, directly fulfilling urgent MBS item criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed topical miconazole 2% cream combined with hydrocortisone 1% to be applied twice daily to the groin for the acute candidal intertrigo. Advised keeping the area strictly dry using a barrier cream or powder after treatment. Arranged an urgent comprehensive fasting blood panel for tomorrow morning, including fasting plasma glucose, HbA1c, and a fasting lipid profile.
Positive Behavioral Change: By committing to a 20-minute brisk walk immediately after your largest meal of the day (action), you force your muscles to independently pull sugar out of your bloodstream without needing insulin (cause), which directly lowers your blood sugar spikes and prevents progression to full diabetes (effect).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Nil.
DME/Safety (Optional): Nil.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3-5 days to review the fasting blood results, check the resolution of the rash, and discuss intensive lifestyle interventions or metformin initiation.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the redness from the groin rash begins to spread rapidly down the leg, if it becomes hot and swollen, or if the patient develops a fever above 38 C, present to the emergency department or call 000 as this indicates a severe secondary bacterial infection.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The dark patches on your neck, your weight distribution, and the fungal infection strongly suggest your body is struggling to process sugar, a state known as pre-diabetes. A fasting blood test tomorrow is essential to confirm exactly where your blood sugar levels sit.
Clinical Course: Without immediate, aggressive changes to your diet and exercise, this condition will inevitably progress to full Type 2 Diabetes, causing permanent damage to your blood vessels and nerves.

